<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-categorical-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Categorical Data</title>

  <p>
    We said in <xref ref="sec-stat-intro"/>, that the methods of descriptive statistics allow us to summarize data.
    Recall our question about if you (who gets up early) or your roommate (who sleeps until the afternoon) are more typical?
    We considered how we'd keep track of the data in a survey to try to study this question; probably a chart.
    In <xref ref="ex-stat-study-basics-2"/>, only a few rows of the chart are shown. We need to summarize!
    In this section, we'll focus on the data for the categorical varibles.
  </p>
  <p>
    This (completely made up) data is in a spreadsheet that you can generate your own copy of with <url href="https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1FaMNfe6uGnvFsW0dy-jpXOp3cAYSaHiHcdIimYPsuFk/copy">this link</url>.
    You can use 
  </p>
  <p>
    We'll start with the simplest thing, and the most relevant data to the initial question, <q>What proportion of college students are morning people?</q>
  </p>
  <example xml:id="ex-frequency">
    <title>Frequency Table</title>
    <p>
      The following chart is called a <term>frequency table</term><idx><h>frequency table</h></idx>.
    </p>
    <tabular>
      <col right="major"></col>
      <col right="medium"></col>
      <col right="minor"></col>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell>Response</cell>
        <cell>Frequency</cell>
        <cell><line>Relative Frequency</line><line>Proportion</line></cell>
        <cell><line>Relative Frequency</line><line>Percentage</line></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Yes</cell>
        <cell>94</cell>
        <cell>0.376</cell>
        <cell>37.6%</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="medium">
        <cell>No</cell>
        <cell>156</cell>
        <cell>0.624</cell>
        <cell>62.4%</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><alert>TOTAL</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>250</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>1</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>100%</alert></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p>
      With a <alert>binary variable</alert>, the chart is small. If you were presenting this, you might rather just say,
      <q>Out of the 250 students surveyed, 94 (or 37.6%) consider themselves a morning person.</q>
      For another categorical variable with more options, a chart helps keep all the information organized.
    </p>
  </example>

  <p>
    The <term>frequency</term><idx><h>frequency</h></idx> column represents a count of how many times a particular response appears.
    This should always be a whole number.
    Both of the <term>relative frequency</term> columns put that count into the context of the whole.
    It is a good idea to have both in your table.
    You can revisit <xref ref="sec-percent"/> to review the details of the percentage formula and the pros and cons of both absolute and relative change.
    The total of the frequency column should always be the sample size. The relative frequency column should total to 1 or 100%, but can sometimes be a little bit off because of rounding.
  </p>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Generate a frequency table for the various majors of the students in the sample.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        You can use the COUNTIF function in Google Sheets to get the frequency.
        Typing <q>=countif(G2:G251,"Game Design")</q> into a cell and pressing enter will produce 17, the number of students in the sample with a Game Design major.
        This particular function isn't covered in <xref ref="sec-spreadsheets"/>, but a review of that section will likely help you figure out how to modify this to count whatever you want.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We can use the COUNTIF function in the spreadsheet to give us the frequency for each response.
        To compute the relative frequency, divide the frequency by 250.
        TO write the relative frequency as a percentage, move to decimal to the right two places.
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <col right="major"></col>
        <col right="medium"></col>
        <col right="minor"></col>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Response</cell>
          <cell>Frequency</cell>
          <cell><line>Relative Frequency</line><line>Proportion</line></cell>
          <cell><line>Relative Frequency</line><line>Percentage</line></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Nursing</cell>
          <cell>54</cell>
          <cell>0.216</cell>
          <cell>21.6%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Business Administration</cell>
          <cell>39</cell>
          <cell>0.156</cell>
          <cell>15.6%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Communications Media</cell>
          <cell>24</cell>
          <cell>0.096</cell>
          <cell>9.6%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Criminal Justice</cell>
          <cell>23</cell>
          <cell>0.092</cell>
          <cell>9.2%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Psychology</cell>
          <cell>21</cell>
          <cell>0.084</cell>
          <cell>8.4%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Game Design</cell>
          <cell>17</cell>
          <cell>0.068</cell>
          <cell>6.8%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Biology</cell>
          <cell>15</cell>
          <cell>0.06</cell>
          <cell>6%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Interdisciplinary Studies</cell>
          <cell>11</cell>
          <cell>0.044</cell>
          <cell>4.4%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Exercise &amp; Sport Science</cell>
          <cell>11</cell>
          <cell>0.044</cell>
          <cell>4.4%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Engineering Technology</cell>
          <cell>10</cell>
          <cell>0.04</cell>
          <cell>4%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Human Services</cell>
          <cell>10</cell>
          <cell>0.04</cell>
          <cell>4%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Education</cell>
          <cell>9</cell>
          <cell>0.036</cell>
          <cell>3.6%</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell>Other</cell>
          <cell>6</cell>
          <cell>0.024</cell>
          <cell>2.4%</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><alert>TOTAL</alert></cell>
          <cell><alert>250</alert></cell>
          <cell><alert>1</alert></cell>
          <cell><alert>100%</alert></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      <p>
        The last row, <q>Other</q>, groups together the four majors (Computer Science, English Studies, History, and Mathematics) that have particularly low frequencies.
        This is a choice you can make. It hides a bit of information, but turns four rows in the chart into a single row.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise>
    <webwork source="Library/ASU-topics/setStat/dueck2_1.pg" />
  </exercise>

  <p>
    A frequency table can only be used to summarize the data for a single variable.
    Often, we want to know something about how two variables are related.
    In the case of two categorical variables, we can do this in a two-way table.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-two-way-table">
    <title>Two-Way Table</title>    
    <p>
      The following <term>two-way table</term><idx><h>two-way-table</h></idx> compares the whether a student considers themselves a morning person with the number of morning classes they are taking.
      The number in a box counts the number of responses that match the value of both the row and column variable.
    </p>
    <tabular>
      <col right="medium"></col>
      <col right="minor"></col>
      <col right="medium"></col>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Not a Morning Person</cell>
        <cell>Morning Person</cell>
        <cell>Row Totals</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>0 Morning Classes</cell>
        <cell>28</cell>
        <cell>17</cell>
        <cell>45</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>1 Morning Class</cell>
        <cell>29</cell>
        <cell>21</cell>
        <cell>50</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>2 Morning Classes</cell>
        <cell>29</cell>
        <cell>27</cell>
        <cell>56</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>3 Morning Classes</cell>
        <cell>36</cell>
        <cell>21</cell>
        <cell>57</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="medium">
        <cell>4 Morning Classes</cell>
        <cell>34</cell>
        <cell>8</cell>
        <cell>42</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><alert>Column Totals</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>156</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>94</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>250</alert></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p>
      In this case, we're treating discrete numerical data as ordinal categorical data.
      We can always do this, but it may not always be a great idea.
      If there are too many options, so that a resulting table would have too many rows or columns, we can group values together.
    </p>
  </example>

  <p>
    
